1
00:00:03,060 --> 00:00:07,924
The request came in at twenty past eleven, on a wet Tuesday night in October,

2
00:00:07,924 --> 00:00:13,103
and I accepted it because I had been parked outside the Harcastle Co-operative for

3
00:00:13,103 --> 00:00:16,640
forty minutes with nothing but a cold flask for company.

4
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:20,673
The pickup pin sat at the far end of Harcastle Road,

5
00:00:20,673 --> 00:00:25,160
where the street lamps give out and the road drops towards the railway cutting.

6
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:27,780
I had never been given a fare there.

7
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:30,020
The estimate was eleven minutes away.

8
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:31,680
I drove it in nine.

9
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:34,813
The rain came in sideways off the allotments,

10
00:00:34,813 --> 00:00:39,281
and the wipers on my Skoda have never cleared more than the middle two-thirds of the

11
00:00:39,281 --> 00:00:39,881
glass.

12
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:43,438
At the underpass I slowed, as I always do,

13
00:00:43,438 --> 00:00:47,100
because the road narrows there and the brick sweats.

14
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:51,907
The work phone sat clamped in its mount on the dashboard,

15
00:00:51,907 --> 00:00:57,304
and the pin on its screen showed a blue dot, then a blue dot with a ring around it,

16
00:00:57,304 --> 00:00:58,800
then the word Arriving.

17
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:01,440
Then the screen froze.

18
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:05,500
I have driven for the app for three years and I know its habits.

19
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:09,307
It stalls in the cutting and under the rail bridge;

20
00:01:09,307 --> 00:01:13,380
it stalls whenever a lorry parks too close to the mast on Fenner Lane.

21
00:01:14,020 --> 00:01:16,020
A frozen screen is nothing.

22
00:01:16,820 --> 00:01:20,191
The map goes grey, the little car stops turning,

23
00:01:20,191 --> 00:01:22,720
and the radio carries on without it.

24
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:25,900
I put the handbrake on and counted, as one does.

25
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:27,100
One.

26
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:27,840
Two.

27
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:29,300
The heater ticked.

28
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:30,380
Three.

29
00:01:30,860 --> 00:01:34,420
Somewhere behind me a drainpipe was emptying itself onto the tarmac.

30
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:38,760
Four, and the display came back, and the rear door was shut.

31
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:40,520
I had not heard it open.

32
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:43,080
I want to be exact about this.

33
00:01:43,820 --> 00:01:47,997
I heard no latch, no change in the weight of the car on its springs,

34
00:01:47,997 --> 00:01:48,980
no rush of rain.

35
00:01:49,820 --> 00:01:52,925
But the interior light was off, as I had left it,

36
00:01:52,925 --> 00:01:56,791
and in the mirror there was a man sitting directly behind me,

37
00:01:56,791 --> 00:01:59,580
hands folded on his knees, a hat on his lap.

38
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:02,060
"Good evening," he said.

39
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:03,640
"I'm sorry to have kept you."

40
00:02:04,580 --> 00:02:05,540
"Mr Drummond?

41
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:08,240
I said, because the app said so.

42
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:10,820
Harold D., 4.9 stars.

43
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:12,280
There was no photograph.

44
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:13,800
"That's right."

45
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:15,920
He was perhaps sixty.

46
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:22,095
His face had the colour of wet slate, not ill, exactly, but finished with colour,

47
00:02:22,095 --> 00:02:23,760
and his raincoat was dry.

48
00:02:24,580 --> 00:02:27,580
I noticed that at once and decided not to notice it.

49
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:30,560
The coat was of a cut my father wore.

50
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:34,880
The hat on his knee was a flat cap, and it was dry as well.

51
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:36,420
"Where are we heading?"

52
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:42,460
He gave me an address: Number nine, Wick Yard, off the Harcastle Road.

53
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:44,100
I typed it.

54
00:02:44,740 --> 00:02:48,100
The app considered it and returned a small red line of text.

55
00:02:48,920 --> 00:02:50,060
Address not found.

56
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:53,360
I tried it without the number, then without the yard.

57
00:02:54,060 --> 00:02:54,660
Nothing.

58
00:02:55,220 --> 00:02:58,860
I said so, apologetically, and offered to use my own map.

59
00:02:59,540 --> 00:03:02,400
"You won't need it," said Mr Drummond.

60
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:03,920
"Keep to the road.

61
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:06,020
I shall tell you.

62
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:07,160
He settled back.

63
00:03:08,220 --> 00:03:10,340
"You mustn't mind the quiet, Mr Whitlow.

64
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:11,940
I'm not much for conversation."

65
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:15,260
I had not told him my name.

66
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:19,558
It was on the dashboard card, of course, in the little plastic window,

67
00:03:19,558 --> 00:03:20,780
and I told myself that.

68
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:23,300
I released the handbrake.

69
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:26,338
The display was still lit, and in the corner,

70
00:03:26,338 --> 00:03:30,240
where the bars of signal should have been, there was a single grey dash.

71
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:36,440
Beside it, in type too small to read, the app had begun to count something upwards.

72
00:03:37,540 --> 00:03:40,060
In the mirror, Mr Drummond was sitting very still.

73
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:44,280
It seemed to me that the back seat was a little narrower than it had been.

74
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:48,660
I drove on because there was nothing else to do.

75
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:53,894
Mr Drummond named the turnings a half-second before I reached them, left here,

76
00:03:53,894 --> 00:03:57,325
now the second right, in a voice with no weight behind it,

77
00:03:57,325 --> 00:04:00,460
and at the end of Wick Yard there was no Number nine.

78
00:04:01,140 --> 00:04:06,158
There was a wall, a drain, and a sodium lamp that had been dead long enough to grow

79
00:04:06,158 --> 00:04:06,758
moss.

80
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:07,900
I stopped.

81
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:11,807
When I turned in my seat the back was empty, the leather dry,

82
00:04:11,807 --> 00:04:13,380
the cushion not even dented.

83
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:17,440
The work phone gave three bars of signal and a rating request.

84
00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:19,340
I did not give one.

85
00:04:20,040 --> 00:04:22,420
On Wednesday the account asked for me again.

86
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:23,840
Harold D.

87
00:04:23,980 --> 00:04:26,455
The pickup was the Harcastle Road underpass,

88
00:04:26,455 --> 00:04:29,324
where the concrete takes every bar from the screen,

89
00:04:29,324 --> 00:04:31,180
and I told myself I would cancel.

90
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:34,200
My thumb was on the button when everything stopped.

91
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:36,420
The picture did not go black.

92
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:42,980
It held, bright and perfectly still, and the clock in the corner stopped at 22:41.

93
00:04:42,980 --> 00:04:44,500
I heard the rear door close.

94
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:46,360
Nothing had opened it.

95
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:48,920
"Good evening, Mr Whitlow."

96
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:53,640
He sat behind the passenger headrest now, no longer in the middle.

97
00:04:54,240 --> 00:04:58,480
I could see the flat cap in the corner of the mirror, and one grey ear.

98
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:01,520
"Mr Drummond," I said.

99
00:05:02,120 --> 00:05:03,000
"What is it you want?

100
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:04,440
Where is it you want to go?

101
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:07,000
He said, "Keep to the road.

102
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:10,960
The clock moved. 22:42.

103
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:15,780
I drove him through four miles of wet streets that led, as before, to nothing.

104
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:17,980
He spoke only to direct me.

105
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:20,935
When I asked a third time what he wanted,

106
00:05:20,935 --> 00:05:25,040
he looked out of the window as a man does who has been asked something rude.

107
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:30,303
On Thursday I took the pickup in the car park behind the Harcastle Infirmary,

108
00:05:30,303 --> 00:05:32,727
which has been shuttered since the spring,

109
00:05:32,727 --> 00:05:37,112
with plywood across the casualty doors and a single fox watching me from the

110
00:05:37,112 --> 00:05:37,920
ambulance bay.

111
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:41,940
I had decided to keep both hands on the wheel and not turn.

112
00:05:42,860 --> 00:05:44,500
The display froze again.

113
00:05:45,340 --> 00:05:46,000
I counted.

114
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:50,165
At four seconds the cold arrived at my right shoulder,

115
00:05:50,165 --> 00:05:53,579
the way a draught arrives from a door you thought was shut,

116
00:05:53,579 --> 00:05:56,820
and a hand came down upon the console between the seats.

117
00:05:57,620 --> 00:06:01,980
It was a large hand, grey as the face, the nails cut square.

118
00:06:02,780 --> 00:06:07,316
It lay beside the gear stick with the fingers a little spread, patient,

119
00:06:07,316 --> 00:06:09,680
exactly where a driver rests his own.

120
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:13,000
There was a gold ring on it, worn thin.

121
00:06:13,880 --> 00:06:19,091
A faint smell came with it, and I knew it before I could name it: engine oil,

122
00:06:19,091 --> 00:06:22,340
and pipe tobacco, and the inside of an old coat.

123
00:06:23,240 --> 00:06:28,040
"Mr Drummond," I said, and my voice did something I had not permitted it to do.

124
00:06:28,820 --> 00:06:29,420
"Please.

125
00:06:29,560 --> 00:06:30,980
What do you want?"

126
00:06:31,310 --> 00:06:32,180
He did not answer.

127
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:34,320
He was looking, I think, at the wheel.

128
00:06:34,980 --> 00:06:37,680
I could feel him looking at the place where my hands were.

129
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:42,600
The picture thawed. 23:17.

130
00:06:42,600 --> 00:06:47,033
A single bar of signal came back, and the app, with its usual cheerfulness,

131
00:06:47,033 --> 00:06:49,220
congratulated me on a ride completed.

132
00:06:50,020 --> 00:06:51,840
The hand was still on the console.

133
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:56,440
It stayed there for the whole drive home, and I did not once look down.

134
00:06:57,320 --> 00:07:01,371
When I parked outside Alder Close and switched off the engine,

135
00:07:01,371 --> 00:07:04,115
I sat a long minute listening to the rain,

136
00:07:04,115 --> 00:07:07,709
and then I heard the rear door open and close, quietly,

137
00:07:07,709 --> 00:07:10,780
and I understood that he had stepped out first.

138
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:14,780
I did not telephone anyone that night.

139
00:07:15,280 --> 00:07:19,701
I sat in the kitchen with the lights on and the back door locked and a cup of tea I

140
00:07:19,701 --> 00:07:20,340
never drank.

141
00:07:21,220 --> 00:07:23,473
At some point after two the cup was cold,

142
00:07:23,473 --> 00:07:26,440
and I found I had been watching the chair opposite me.

143
00:07:27,160 --> 00:07:31,278
In the morning I rang the driver support line from the bottom of the garden,

144
00:07:31,278 --> 00:07:33,120
standing among the wet bean poles.

145
00:07:33,820 --> 00:07:38,467
It seemed to me that a thing which could follow a car might not follow a signal as

146
00:07:38,467 --> 00:07:39,260
far as a shed.

147
00:07:39,940 --> 00:07:45,380
I used the house phone, the cordless one, carried down the path with its aerial out.

148
00:07:45,960 --> 00:07:48,412
The work phone, the one that ran the app,

149
00:07:48,412 --> 00:07:51,880
I had left in my coat pocket on the hook by the shed door.

150
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:53,280
There was a menu.

151
00:07:53,900 --> 00:07:56,860
There was a recording that told me my call was important.

152
00:07:57,680 --> 00:08:01,827
After eleven minutes a young woman took my driver number and my name,

153
00:08:01,827 --> 00:08:06,756
asked me to describe the issue, and listened to the description with the courteous

154
00:08:06,756 --> 00:08:09,220
blankness of someone reading from a card.

155
00:08:10,040 --> 00:08:12,200
She said she would put me through to a supervisor.

156
00:08:13,060 --> 00:08:14,720
The line clicked twice.

157
00:08:16,240 --> 00:08:18,200
"Hargreaves," said a man.

158
00:08:18,780 --> 00:08:19,680
"Vernon Hargreaves.

159
00:08:20,040 --> 00:08:21,060
Go ahead, Mr Whitlow."

160
00:08:22,420 --> 00:08:26,760
His voice was flat and unhurried, a voice with the edges planed off.

161
00:08:27,720 --> 00:08:33,611
I told him everything: the Tuesday request, the four seconds, the man in the back,

162
00:08:33,611 --> 00:08:37,060
the three nights since, the hand on the console.

163
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:39,960
I said the account name twice, and spelled it.

164
00:08:39,960 --> 00:08:44,520
I heard him typing, slow, two fingers by the sound of it.

165
00:08:45,940 --> 00:08:48,520
"There's no Drummond," he said.

166
00:08:49,160 --> 00:08:50,980
"Not as a rider, not as a driver.

167
00:08:51,540 --> 00:08:56,640
No account under that name, no trips to or from Alder Close with that profile.

168
00:08:57,580 --> 00:09:00,340
I've pulled your history for the last fourteen days.

169
00:09:01,100 --> 00:09:06,040
Every fare you've taken is matched to a named passenger with a payment on file."

170
00:09:06,980 --> 00:09:09,740
"Then look at Harcastle Road," I said.

171
00:09:10,240 --> 00:09:10,920
"The underpass.

172
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:13,100
The car park behind the infirmary."

173
00:09:14,120 --> 00:09:14,900
More typing.

174
00:09:15,540 --> 00:09:20,583
Somewhere behind him a door closed, and the sound of it went on a little too long,

175
00:09:20,583 --> 00:09:23,720
as if the room were larger than a room ought to be.

176
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:28,380
"What I have," said Vernon, "is you.

177
00:09:29,160 --> 00:09:31,640
You've been logged in for six hours, Mr Whitlow.

178
00:09:32,280 --> 00:09:34,120
Same stretch of Harcastle Road.

179
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:39,640
Your location hasn't moved since eleven last night, and your status has not changed.

180
00:09:40,340 --> 00:09:42,680
It still shows you available.

181
00:09:43,120 --> 00:09:43,720
He paused.

182
00:09:44,420 --> 00:09:45,460
"Are you at the car now?"

183
00:09:46,280 --> 00:09:47,360
"I'm in my garden."

184
00:09:48,200 --> 00:09:49,760
"Yes," he said.

185
00:09:50,300 --> 00:09:51,440
"That's what's odd about it."

186
00:09:52,400 --> 00:09:56,480
I waited for him to suggest a doctor, or a rest.

187
00:09:56,480 --> 00:09:57,320
He did neither.

188
00:09:58,120 --> 00:10:03,370
The typing had stopped, and the line carried only the thin sound of an open

189
00:10:03,370 --> 00:10:06,240
connection, the sound of a long corridor.

190
00:10:07,240 --> 00:10:09,060
I counted nine seconds.

191
00:10:09,700 --> 00:10:12,397
I was going to say his name when he spoke again,

192
00:10:12,397 --> 00:10:14,420
and his voice had lost its flatness.

193
00:10:15,520 --> 00:10:16,780
It had become careful.

194
00:10:17,760 --> 00:10:20,400
"I'm going to tell you something that is not in the script.

195
00:10:21,160 --> 00:10:22,480
I looked at the wrong screen.

196
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:24,380
The account exists.

197
00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:29,400
It was never deleted, only flagged, and I am looking at the flag now.

198
00:10:30,380 --> 00:10:31,240
A breath.

199
00:10:32,180 --> 00:10:33,620
"Harold Drummond.

200
00:10:33,620 --> 00:10:37,050
He drove a cab on Harcastle Road for twenty-two years,

201
00:10:37,050 --> 00:10:41,560
and in November 1987 he went out on a wet night and was not seen again.

202
00:10:42,300 --> 00:10:43,900
The cab was never found.

203
00:10:44,520 --> 00:10:47,360
The account was opened for him afterwards, Mr Whitlow.

204
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:48,620
Opened.

205
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,080
Nobody here can tell me by whom."

206
00:10:51,080 --> 00:10:56,980
"That is not possible," I said, and heard how little it was worth.

207
00:10:57,840 --> 00:10:58,980
"There's a note on the flag.

208
00:10:59,680 --> 00:11:03,560
Head office has been told that the application fails in one circumstance only.

209
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:08,340
When a ride is marked completed that was never started.

210
00:11:08,880 --> 00:11:11,040
The corridor sound swelled behind him.

211
00:11:11,040 --> 00:11:15,360
"Mr Whitlow, did your app congratulate you last night?"

212
00:11:16,040 --> 00:11:17,200
I did not answer.

213
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:20,463
Down by the fence the bean poles ticked in the wind,

214
00:11:20,463 --> 00:11:24,731
and in the coat on the hook the work phone, which I had not touched,

215
00:11:24,731 --> 00:11:26,300
gave a single soft chime.

216
00:11:28,680 --> 00:11:30,420
I did not answer the supervisor.

217
00:11:31,180 --> 00:11:36,600
I let the line run on, the corridor sound and Vernon Hargreaves breathing in it.

218
00:11:37,060 --> 00:11:40,880
I took the work phone out of the coat on the hook and read what it said.

219
00:11:41,760 --> 00:11:42,800
Trip completed.

220
00:11:43,640 --> 00:11:45,220
Thank you for driving, Douglas.

221
00:11:45,880 --> 00:11:47,740
You earned £0.00.

222
00:11:47,740 --> 00:11:52,520
There was a rating beneath it, five stars, and no name against it.

223
00:11:53,400 --> 00:11:54,300
"Mr Whitlow?"

224
00:11:55,040 --> 00:11:55,680
"I'm here."

225
00:11:56,660 --> 00:11:57,840
"Then I'll say the rest of it.

226
00:11:58,400 --> 00:12:01,440
The logs show your app has dropped out eleven times in three nights.

227
00:12:02,040 --> 00:12:05,160
Each time, directly afterwards, the trip is marked complete.

228
00:12:06,020 --> 00:12:07,440
Nobody has ever started one.

229
00:12:08,240 --> 00:12:09,280
He cleared his throat.

230
00:12:10,080 --> 00:12:12,020
"I would not take another request."

231
00:12:13,420 --> 00:12:14,320
I thanked him.

232
00:12:15,120 --> 00:12:16,060
I ended the call.

233
00:12:16,820 --> 00:12:19,914
I stood among the bean poles with the rain beginning again,

234
00:12:19,914 --> 00:12:24,320
and I laid the three nights out in a row, as I would have laid out a week's takings.

235
00:12:25,560 --> 00:12:27,240
He had never wanted to go anywhere.

236
00:12:28,240 --> 00:12:29,940
That was the thing I had missed.

237
00:12:30,720 --> 00:12:34,089
He had given me an address that was not in the system,

238
00:12:34,089 --> 00:12:37,396
and an address that does not exist cannot be reached,

239
00:12:37,396 --> 00:12:39,580
and so the journey could never end.

240
00:12:40,560 --> 00:12:43,320
That was why he had never once asked me to hurry.

241
00:12:43,760 --> 00:12:46,520
He had asked me only to be easy with the silence.

242
00:12:47,260 --> 00:12:50,124
He had moved from the back seat to the headrest,

243
00:12:50,124 --> 00:12:54,240
and from the headrest to my shoulder, one reach of the arm at a time.

244
00:12:54,960 --> 00:13:00,458
Each reach had been made in the four or five seconds when the display froze and the

245
00:13:00,458 --> 00:13:02,180
road stopped being a road.

246
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:04,920
I had taken those seconds for faults.

247
00:13:04,920 --> 00:13:08,950
They were the only seconds in which the car belonged to nobody,

248
00:13:08,950 --> 00:13:12,660
and he had been using them as a man uses a gap in a hedge.

249
00:13:13,460 --> 00:13:14,680
He was not a passenger.

250
00:13:15,200 --> 00:13:19,179
He was a driver who had lost his seat in 1987, on a wet night,

251
00:13:19,179 --> 00:13:24,120
on that same stretch of tarmac, and he was working his way back to the wheel.

252
00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:26,860
I ought to have deleted the application.

253
00:13:27,380 --> 00:13:28,800
I ought to have sold the car.

254
00:13:29,540 --> 00:13:32,530
I did neither, and I have thought since about why,

255
00:13:32,530 --> 00:13:35,700
and the honest answer is that I wanted to be certain.

256
00:13:36,320 --> 00:13:39,620
A man will walk into a good deal for the sake of certainty.

257
00:13:40,700 --> 00:13:45,409
At ten past eleven that night, with the work phone clipped back into its mount on

258
00:13:45,409 --> 00:13:46,920
the dash, it chimed again.

259
00:13:47,960 --> 00:13:48,500
New request.

260
00:13:48,500 --> 00:13:52,800
Harcastle Road underpass. 4 min.

261
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:54,000
The account name was blank.

262
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:57,120
I drove there with the wipers on their slowest setting.

263
00:13:57,740 --> 00:14:01,030
The underpass is two hundred yards of brick and sodium light,

264
00:14:01,030 --> 00:14:05,399
with a drain at the lowest point that has been overflowing since the council last

265
00:14:05,399 --> 00:14:06,100
mentioned it.

266
00:14:07,060 --> 00:14:11,140
I stopped at the mouth, set the handbrake, and looked down into it.

267
00:14:12,120 --> 00:14:15,580
The signal bar on the screen had already shrunk to a single stroke.

268
00:14:16,600 --> 00:14:17,840
I watched it flicker.

269
00:14:18,540 --> 00:14:22,420
The back seat was empty, and the mirror showed me only my own face.

270
00:14:23,260 --> 00:14:24,660
I turned the heater off.

271
00:14:25,160 --> 00:14:27,720
I did not want warmth to confuse what came.

272
00:14:28,520 --> 00:14:34,317
Then I pressed the button on the side of the handset and held it until it asked

273
00:14:34,317 --> 00:14:39,600
whether I was sure, and I said aloud, "Yes," as if someone had asked me.

274
00:14:40,420 --> 00:14:41,640
The screen went black.

275
00:14:42,480 --> 00:14:46,620
The little blue light on the mount, which had been lit for three years, went out.

276
00:14:47,340 --> 00:14:51,752
I released the handbrake, put the car into gear and rolled forward into the

277
00:14:51,752 --> 00:14:52,352
underpass.

278
00:14:53,020 --> 00:14:57,640
Behind me, with no sound of a door, the rear seat took a man's weight and settled.

279
00:14:59,580 --> 00:15:03,220
The hand on mine was cold in a way I can only call dry.

280
00:15:03,720 --> 00:15:07,920
There was no damp in it, though the whole city outside was running with rain.

281
00:15:08,700 --> 00:15:13,436
It lay over my knuckles on the gear stick and the weight of it was exact,

282
00:15:13,436 --> 00:15:18,691
each finger placed, as a man might rest his hand on a thing he had owned for many

283
00:15:18,691 --> 00:15:19,291
years.

284
00:15:19,960 --> 00:15:21,320
I had thought I would speak.

285
00:15:21,940 --> 00:15:26,427
I had prepared a sentence in the car park at home, something about what he wanted,

286
00:15:26,427 --> 00:15:30,860
and whether he could say it plainly now that there was no signal to interrupt us.

287
00:15:31,400 --> 00:15:32,560
I did not say it.

288
00:15:33,100 --> 00:15:34,220
The engine idled.

289
00:15:34,840 --> 00:15:38,740
The dashboard, which I had not touched, lost its brightness by degrees.

290
00:15:39,600 --> 00:15:45,220
First went the clock, then the fuel gauge, then the small orange lamp for the oil.

291
00:15:46,220 --> 00:15:51,544
After that the only light came from the sodium lamps at the mouth of the underpass,

292
00:15:51,544 --> 00:15:56,740
and then not even that, for the car had rolled further in than I had meant it to.

293
00:15:57,660 --> 00:16:00,994
The seat behind me creaked as it gave up its weight,

294
00:16:00,994 --> 00:16:03,879
and the seat beside me creaked as it took it,

295
00:16:03,879 --> 00:16:06,380
though I had heard no one pass between.

296
00:16:06,380 --> 00:16:08,880
I did not look.

297
00:16:09,340 --> 00:16:12,964
I looked at the windscreen, at the brick curving overhead,

298
00:16:12,964 --> 00:16:15,400
at the water running down to the drain.

299
00:16:16,280 --> 00:16:21,802
I could smell old upholstery and the sweetish stale smell of a cab that has been

300
00:16:21,802 --> 00:16:23,320
smoked in for decades.

301
00:16:24,360 --> 00:16:26,240
My own car had never smelled of either.

302
00:16:27,060 --> 00:16:31,160
"Mind the quiet," said a voice beside me, mild and dry.

303
00:16:31,920 --> 00:16:32,520
"Most don't."

304
00:16:33,580 --> 00:16:35,060
The hand lifted from mine.

305
00:16:35,680 --> 00:16:40,374
I heard the click of a seat belt, mine, being undone, and I felt myself rise,

306
00:16:40,374 --> 00:16:44,580
or be raised, with no more effort than a coat being taken from a peg.

307
00:16:45,360 --> 00:16:48,730
There was a draught of cold air, a door that did not open,

308
00:16:48,730 --> 00:16:53,493
and the sound of the gear lever being worked by someone who knew exactly how stiff

309
00:16:53,493 --> 00:16:54,093
it was.

310
00:16:54,820 --> 00:16:59,540
Then the screen in its mount, dead a moment before, lit by itself.

311
00:17:00,780 --> 00:17:04,140
The light was white and very bright after the dark.

312
00:17:04,960 --> 00:17:06,900
I had to blink before I could read it.

313
00:17:07,540 --> 00:17:11,056
I was sitting upright in the back seat, behind the passenger side,

314
00:17:11,056 --> 00:17:13,560
where the vinyl was cracked in a long diagonal.

315
00:17:14,440 --> 00:17:19,767
Across my chest ran the belt, drawn snug and clicked home by hands that were not

316
00:17:19,767 --> 00:17:20,367
mine.

317
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:22,100
I tried my arms.

318
00:17:22,600 --> 00:17:23,200
They moved.

319
00:17:23,720 --> 00:17:25,120
I did not try the door.

320
00:17:25,780 --> 00:17:29,323
In the driver's seat sat a grey head, bare and narrow,

321
00:17:29,323 --> 00:17:33,260
the hair combed flat with something that had dried long ago.

322
00:17:34,060 --> 00:17:36,460
Two hands rested on the wheel at ten and two.

323
00:17:37,400 --> 00:17:40,420
The wipers began, slowly, on their own.

324
00:17:41,140 --> 00:17:46,543
Beyond the glass the underpass was no longer an underpass but a road I did not know,

325
00:17:46,543 --> 00:17:50,080
lit and empty, and the rain fell on it without a sound.

326
00:17:53,840 --> 00:17:54,240
The mirror showed me nothing at all.

327
00:17:54,240 --> 00:17:57,080
Only the screen spoke, in its small clean letters:

328
00:17:58,120 --> 00:17:58,720
Trip started.

329
00:17:58,720 --> 00:18:01,720
Pickup: 14 Alder Close.

330
00:18:02,340 --> 00:18:03,000
It was my address.

331
00:18:03,660 --> 00:18:07,660
The indicator ticked, left, left, and Harold Drummond drove me home.

